"Discrete Mathematics 344 (2021) 112332 (https://idus.us.es/server/api/core/bitstreams/1de399e2-12bc-4b46-8342-5923b1ba28e2/content)\nciteturn9search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.6 years ago; A recursive upper bound on S(n)? ... To the best of our knowledge, no recursive upper bounds are known yet for the Schur numbers.\nS. Eliahou and M.P. Revuelta\nDiscrete Mathematics 344 (2021) 112332\n5. A recursive upper bound on S(n)?\nThe Ramsey numbers admit well-known recursive upper bounds, including\nRn(3) ≤n(Rn−1(3) −1) + 2\n(10)\n[8, Theorem 6, p. 6]. To the best of our knowledge, no recursive upper bounds are known yet for the Schur numbers.\nWe propose here a conjecture which, if true, would fill this gap. Let us start with an upper bound on S(n) involving the\nnumber L(n) defined below.\nDefinition 5.1.\nLet n ≥2. We define L(n) to be the smallest positive integer with the following property: for every\nsequence A in N+ of length |A| = L(n) and average µ(A) ≤n, one has sdeg(ˆA) ≥n.\nExample 5.2.\nLet n = 2. Then L(2) = 2. Indeed, up to symmetry, the only sequences A to consider are (1, 1), (1, 2), (1, 3),\n(2, 2). This yields ˆA = {1, 2}, {1, 2, 3}, {1, 3, 4}, {2, 4}, respectively. As none is sumfree, we have sdeg(ˆA) ≥2 in all cases,\nas required.\nLet us now establish the existence of L(n) in full generality.\nProposition 5.3.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe conjectural recursive upper bound for Schur numbers | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/346739/the-conjectural-recursive-upper-bound-for-schur-numbers)\nciteturn9search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; # The conjectural recursive upper bound for Schur numbers ... The recursive Schur-number upper-bound conjecture.\n\n# The conjectural recursive upper bound for Schur numbers\n\nAbout 6 years old · traced to 2020\n\nFor $n\\ge 2$n≥2, let $S(n)$S(n) denote the $n$n th Schur number. The recursive Schur-number upper-bound conjecture.\n\n$$S(n)\\le n\\bigl(S(n-1)+1\\bigr)\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge 2.$$S(n)≤n(S(n−1)+1)for all n≥2.\n\nThis conjecture follows from the preceding conjecture on $L(n)$L(n) together with the paper's upper-bound theorem, and it is verified in the source for $2\\le n\\le 5$2≤n≤5.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nShalom Eliahou and Pastora Revuelta, “The Schur degree of additive sets”, arXiv:2006.01502 (2020).\n\nadditive-combinatoricsschur-numbers\n\n0 0\n\nPosted 2 months ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLower and upper bounds for Schur numbers: AI research tracker · AIProofWatch (https://aiproofwatch.com/problems/schur-number-bounds)\nciteturn9search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; What counts as progress: A verified larger sum-free coloring, a stronger recursion, or a proved upper bound; any separate Ramsey premise must remain explicit. ... Hanson Improved recursive lower bounds for Schur numbers using generalized constructions.\n\n# Lower and upper bounds for Schur numbers\n\nThe Schur number S(k) is the largest N for which {1,…,N} admits a k-coloring with no monochromatic solution to x+y=z. How can recursive constructions and obstructions improve its lower and upper bounds?\n\nWhy it matters and what counts as progress\n\nWhy it matters: A better recurrence produces stronger finite bounds, while conditional upper bounds expose how Ramsey information constrains Schur colorings.\n\nWhat counts as progress: A verified larger sum-free coloring, a stronger recursion, or a proved upper bound; any separate Ramsey premise must remain explicit.\n\n## How the problem developed\n\n  1. 1972 · Lower bound [1]\n\nH. L. Abbott and D. Hanson Improved recursive lower bounds for Schur numbers using generalized constructions.\n\n  2. 26 Apr 2018 · Exact value [2]\n\nMarijn J. H. Heule Determined the exact value S(5)=160 with a machine-checkable SAT proof.\n\n  3. 16 Jul 2026 · Lower bound [3]\n\nLuca Bengone, Julien Brouk, Pierre Grinsztajn, Luca Helbert, Alessio Lugherini, Pierre Rimmel and Martin Tomasik Improved the recursive lower bound to S(k+2)≥10S(k)+2, yielding S(8)≥5362 and S(13)≥2,038,282; the paper credits AI search with discovering the construction and human work with refinement.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFinding and counting small tournaments in large tournaments (https://math.haifa.ac.il/raphy/papers/k-count-tour.pdf)\nciteturn9search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; well-studied counting problem for undirected cliques of order k −O(log k). ... Keywords: tournament; counting; detection ... research in algorithmic graph theory (see Subsection 1.2) and our goal is to investigate these ... For a tournament T, let c(T) denote the infimum over all reals t such that T-COUNT can be\nFinding and counting small tournaments in large tournaments\nRaphael Yuster *\nAbstract\nWe present new algorithms for counting and detecting small tournaments in a given tour-\nnament. In particular, we prove that every tournament on four vertices (there are four) can be\ndetected in O(n2) time and counted in O(nω) time where ω < 2.372 is the matrix multiplication\nexponent. We further prove that any tournament on five vertices (there are 12) can be counted\nin O(nω+1) time. As for lower-bounds, we prove that for almost all k-vertex tournaments, the\ncomplexity of the detection problem is not easier than the complexity of the corresponding\nwell-studied counting problem for undirected cliques of order k −O(log k).\n2012 ACM Subject Classification: Theory of computation →Design and analysis of algo-\nrithms →Graph algorithms analysis\nKeywords: tournament; counting; detection\n1\nIntroduction\nTournaments, which are orientations of complete graphs, are well-studied objects in combinatorics,\nalgorithmic graph theory, and computational social choice; see [4] for a large body of references in all\nof these areas. This paper considers the basic problem of detecting and counting small tournaments\nin larger tournaments. Detecting and counting small graphs in larger graphs are major topics of\nresearch in algorithmic graph theory (see Subsection 1.2) and our goal is to investigate these\nproblems from the tournament perspective.\nWe usually denote small (fixed size) tournaments by T, and large tournaments (problem in-\nstances) by G. The unique transitive tournament on k vertices is denoted by Tk. For a vertex v\nin a tournament G, d+\nG(v) denotes its out-degree and d−\nG(v) denotes its in-degree; the subscript is\nomitted if clear from context. Our main problems are:\nT-DETECT. For a fixed tournament T, determine if an input tournament contains a copy of T\nas a subgraph.\nT-COUNT. For a fixed tournament T, count the number of copies of T in an input tournament.\nFor a tournament T, let c(T) denote the infimum over all reals t such that T-COUNT can be\ncomputed in O(nt) time and let d(T) denote the infimum over all reals t such that T-DETECT can\nbe solved in O(nt) time. Let c(k) (resp. d(k)) be the maximum of c(T) (resp. d(T)) ranging over\nall k-vertex tournaments. Finally, let c∗(k) and d∗(k) denote the corresponding exponents for the\ncounting and detection problems of Kk (the complete graph on k vertices) in undirected graphs.\n*Department of Mathematics, University of Haifa, Haifa 3498838, Israel. Email: raphael.yuster@gmail.com .\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAn Abstract Procedure to Compute Weak Schur Number Lower Bounds (https://helios2.mi.parisdescartes.fr/~bouzy/publications/bouzy-weakSchur-tr-lipade.pdf)\nciteturn9search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.1 years ago; Our method is recursive, based on the principle that good solutions to the K + 1 column problem are extensions of good solutions to the K column problem. ... The Schur problem [1] consists in finding K partitions of the interval [1, n] such that, in any partition, no number is the sum of any other numbers in that partition. It was proved that, for K partitions, and R operands of the sum, n has an upper bound, called the Schur number S(K, R).\nAn Abstract Procedure to Compute Weak Schur Number Lower Bounds\n\nBruno Bouzy¹\n\n¹ LIPADE, Paris Descartes University, France\n\nAbstract\n\nIn this paper, we present a new method to compute explicit solutions to the weak Schur problem, and consequently obtain lower bounds to weak Schur numbers WS(K) for K ≤ 8. Our method is recursive, based on the principle that good solutions to the K + 1 column problem are extensions of good solutions to the K column problem. We make several observations on the regularities of good solutions: (1) the last column is filled with nearly consecutive numbers. (2) The beginning of a column may contain holes. We test an abstract recursive function with refinements based on these observations. We define and implement an abstract simulation usable by Monte-Carlo search. Consequently, our algorithms search solutions in an abstract space whose size is several orders of magnitude smaller than the original space. Using such tools, we discovered new bounds: WS(7) ≥ 1736 and WS(8) ≥ 5105.\n\nKeywords: Weak Schur numbers, MAX Problems, Planning, Search\n\n1. Introduction\n\nThe Schur problem [1] consists in finding K partitions of the interval [1, n] such that, in any partition, no number is the sum of any other numbers in that partition. It was proved that, for K partitions, and R operands of the sum, n has an upper bound, called the Schur number S(K, R). When the sum is restricted to distinct numbers, this is\n\nEmail address: bruno.bouzy@parisdescartes.fr (Bruno Bouzy)\n\nLIPADE