Button: Prev][Button: 1][Button: 2][Button: 3][Button: Next]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA revival of the girth conjecture - ScienceDirect (https://doi.org/10.1016%2Fj.jctb.2004.04.003)\nciteturn9search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; # A revival of the girth conjecture ... 10.1016/j.jctb.2004.04.003 ... We show that for each ε>0, there exists a number g such that the circular chromatic index of every cubic bridgeless graph with girth at least g is at most 3+ε.\n\n## Journal of Combinatorial Theory, Series B\n\nDate: September 2004\n\nPages: 41-53\n\nVolume: Volume 92, Issue 1\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\n# A revival of the girth conjecture\n\nAuthor links open overlay panelTomáš Kaiser ^{a} ^{c}, Daniel Král’ ^{b} ^{c}, Riste Škrekovski ^{c} ^{d}\n\nShow more\n\nView PDFDownload full issue\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/j.jctb.2004.04.003\n\nMore actions\n\n  * Article\n  * Recommended articles\n  * Cited by (15)\n  * Metrics\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe show that for each ε>0, there exists a number g such that the circular chromatic index of every cubic bridgeless graph with girth at least g is at most 3+ε. This contrasts to the fact (which disproved the Girth conjecture) that there are snarks of arbitrarily large girth. In particular, we show that every cubic bridgeless graph with girth at least 14 has the circular chromatic index at most 7/2.\n\n## Keywords\n\nEdge coloring\n\n;\n\nCircular coloring\n\n;\n\nSnark\n\n  * Previous article in this issue--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nClaude-assisted Schur-degree results refute a block-sum conjecture · AIProofWatch (https://aiproofwatch.com/updates/claude-assisted-schur-degree-results-refute-a-block-sum-conjecture-824bb45c)\nciteturn9search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; Crawled: last week; Their separate recursive conjectures about Schur numbers remain open. ... The stronger paper-only upper bound L(5)≤61 was not formalized and is excluded from this assessment.\n\n# Claude-assisted Schur-degree results refute a block-sum conjecture\n\n  * Mathematics\n  * Theoretical Computer Science\n\nL(4)=16 and L(5)≥49 for the Schur degree of block sums. The two results refute Eliahou–Revuelta Conjecture 5.6 at n=4 and n=5.\n\n## Sources for this record\n\n  * Original claimSchur-degree paper and baselinegithub.com\n  * Supporting artifactProof artifacts and AI provenancegithub.com\n  * Supporting artifactLean comparator check and scopegithub.com\n\n## What was already known\n\nEliahou and Revuelta had 14≤L(4)≤16 and conjectured L(n)=S(n−1)+1, predicting L(4)=14 and L(5)=45. Their separate recursive conjectures about Schur numbers remain open.\n\n## AI involvement AI-led\n\nAdam McKenna reports that Claude Opus 5.5 produced the explicit sequence, group-partition lift, proofs, programs and Lean formalization under his direction; independent Claude agents audited them. McKenna is credited with directing and publishing the project.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProblems in Graph Theory and Combinatorics (https://www.dwest.web.illinois.edu/openp/index.html)\nciteturn9search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3.3 years ago; This site was intended as a resource for research in graph theory and combinatorics but is now long neglected. ... The circular chromatic number χ_{c} of G is the minimum ratio k/d such that G has a (k,d)-coloring; the ceiling of this is the ordinary chromatic number. ...   * Jaeger's Conjecture, special case (every planar graph with girth at least 4k is C_{2k+1}-colorable) ...   * Nedela-Skoviera Conjecture (every irreducible snark has girth at most 6)\n\n#### Graph Homomorphism and Circular Coloring\n\nA homomorphism from a graph G to a graph H is a function f: V(G)→V(H) such that the images of adjacent vertices are adjacent. A (k,d)-coloring of G is an assignment of congruence classes modulo k to vertices of G such that adjacent vertices have colors differing by at least d. Such an assignment is a homomorphism from G into the complement of the dth power of a k-cycle. The circular chromatic number χ_{c} of G is the minimum ratio k/d such that G has a (k,d)-coloring; the ceiling of this is the ordinary chromatic number. A homomorphism from G into H is an H-coloring of G.\n\n  * Jaeger's Conjecture, special case (every planar graph with girth at least 4k is C_{2k+1}-colorable)\n  * Albertson-Chan-Haas Problem (is it true that every n-vertex graph with odd girth at least 2k+1 and minimum degree greater than 3n/(4k) is C_{2k+1}-colorable?)\n\n#### Generalized Coloring\n\n#### Stronger Coloring Requirements\n\n  * L(2,1)-labeling (every graph with maximum degree k can be colored with colors 0,...,k^{2} such that the colors on adjacent vertices differ by at least 2 and the colors on vertices at distance 2 differ by at least 1)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCircular choosability - Havet - 2009 - Journal of Graph Theory - Wiley Online Library (https://doi.org/10.1002/jgt.20375)\nciteturn9search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 17.6 years ago; Crawled: last month; The research in this paper was carried out while the second and third authors were doctoral research students at the University of Oxford and the last author was a doctoral research student at MASCOTTE. ... Mohar asked for the value of τ ≔ sup{cch(G) : G is planar}, and we prove that 6≤τ≤8, thereby providing a negative answer to another question of Mohar. We also study the circular choice number of planar and outerplanar graphs with prescribed girth, and graphs with bounded density. ... J Graph Theory 61: 241–270, 2009\n\nFrédéric Havet\n\n  * fhavet@sophia.inria.fr\n\nMASCOTTE, I3S (CNRS-UNSA)-INRIA, 2004 Route Des Lucioles, BP 93, 06902 Sophia Antipolis Cedex, France\n\nSearch for more papers by this author\n\n\nFirst published: 13 March 2009\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1002/jgt.20375Digital Object Identifier (DOI)\n\nview metrics\n\n^{†}\n\nThe research in this paper was carried out while the second and third authors were doctoral research students at the University of Oxford and the last author was a doctoral research student at MASCOTTE.\n\n### Abstract\n\nWe study circular choosability, a notion recently introduced by Mohar and Zhu. First, we provide a negative answer to a question of Zhu about circular cliques. We next prove that cch(G)=O(ch(G)+ln|V(G)|) for every graph G. We investigate a generalization of circular choosability, the circular f-choosability, where f is a function of the degrees. We also consider the circular choice number of planar graphs. Mohar asked for the value of τ ≔ sup{cch(G) : G is planar}, and we prove that 6≤τ≤8, thereby providing a negative answer to another question of Mohar. We also study the circular choice number of planar and outerplanar graphs with prescribed girth, and graphs with bounded density. © 2009 Wiley Periodicals, Inc. J Graph Theory 61: 241–270, 2009\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCircular chromatic number of plan... preview & related info | Mendeley (https://www.mendeley.com/catalogue/e5dfd56f-6de8-3d84-bbb2-c921fd1303ed/)\nciteturn9search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; The restriction of this conjecture to planar graphs is equivalent to the statement that planar graphs of girth at least 4k have circular chromatic number at most 2 + 1/k. ... This paper proves that for k ≥ 2, planar graphs of odd girth at least 8k - 3 have circular chromatic number at most 2 + 1/k.\nJournal Article OPEN ACCESS\n\n# Circular chromatic number of planar graphs of large odd girth\n\n  * Zhu X\n  * Electronic Journal of Combinatorics (2001) 8(1 R) 1-11\n\nDOI: 10.37236/1569\n\n24 Citations\n\nCitations of this article\n\n9 Readers\n\nMendeley users who have this article in their library.\n\nAdd to library View PDF\n\n#### Abstract\n\nIt was conjectured by Jaeger that 4k-edge connected graphs admit a (2k + 1, k)-flow. The restriction of this conjecture to planar graphs is equivalent to the statement that planar graphs of girth at least 4k have circular chromatic number at most 2 + 1/k. Even this restricted version of Jaeger's conjecture is largely open. The k = 1 case is the well-known Grötzsch 3-colour theorem. This paper proves that for k ≥ 2, planar graphs of odd girth at least 8k - 3 have circular chromatic number at most 2 + 1/k.\n\n#### Cite\n\nCITATION STYLE\n\nAPA\n\n[Input]\n\nZhu, X. (2001). Circular chromatic number of planar graphs of large odd girth. Electronic Journal of Combinatorics, 8(1 R), 1–11. https://doi.org/10.37236/1569--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Constructive Lower Bounds on (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/2872996_Constructive_Lower_Bounds_on)\nciteturn9search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 22.4 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; Exoo, A Lower Bound for Schur Numbers and Multicolor Ramsey Num- ... Radziszowski, An Upper Bound of 62 on the\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPPT - Exploring Schur Numbers: Bounds, Groups, and Recursive Strategies PowerPoint Presentation - ID:5848938 (https://www.slideserve.com/oprah-cline/schur-number-of-groups)\nciteturn9search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.7 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Exploring Schur Numbers: Bounds, Groups, and Recursive Strategies ... Recursive Upper Bound • It is easy to see • from a partition • where are sum-free sets of .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) New lower bounds for Schur and weak Schur numbers (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/356816762_New_lower_bounds_for_Schur_and_weak_Schur_numbers)\nciteturn9search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.9 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; Here we give a general lower bound for weak Schur numbers as a func